{"graph_chars": "Put demand and supply on the same graph. Where they cross is equilibrium. For gasoline in the book, that is one dollar forty a gallon and six hundred million gallons. Equilibrium price is the only price where the plans of buyers and the plans of sellers agree. Equilibrium quantity is that common amount. At one dollar eighty, quantity demanded is five hundred, quantity supplied is six hundred eighty. Excess supply: a surplus. Gasoline piles up. Sellers cut price rather than not sell. Price falls toward equilibrium. At one dollar twenty, quantity demanded is seven hundred, quantity supplied is five hundred fifty. Excess demand: a shortage. Buyers mob the stations. Sellers raise price. Price rises toward equilibrium. If the market is at equilibrium, it has no reason to move. If it is not, pressures push it there.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0673], [0.0673, 0.1346], [0.1346, 0.2019], [0.2019, 0.2692], [0.2692, 0.3365], [0.3365, 0.4038], [0.4038, 0.4711], [0.4711, 0.5384], [0.5384, 0.6057], [0.6057, 0.673], [0.673, 0.7403], [0.7403, 0.8076], [0.8076, 0.8748], [0.8748, 0.9421], [0.9421, 1.0094], [1.0094, 1.0767], [1.0767, 1.144], [1.144, 1.2113], [1.2113, 1.2786], [1.2786, 1.3459], [1.3459, 1.4132], [1.4132, 1.4805], [1.4805, 1.5478], [1.5478, 1.6151], [1.6151, 1.6824], [1.6824, 1.7497], [1.7497, 1.817], [1.817, 1.8843], [1.8843, 1.9516], [1.9516, 2.0189], [2.0189, 2.0862], [2.0862, 2.1535], [2.1535, 2.2208], [2.2208, 2.2881], [2.2881, 2.3554], [2.3554, 2.4227], [2.4227, 2.49], [2.49, 2.5572], [2.5572, 2.6245], [2.6245, 2.6918], [2.6918, 2.7591], [2.7591, 2.8264], [2.8264, 2.8937], [2.8937, 2.961], [2.961, 3.0283], [3.0283, 3.0956], [3.0956, 3.1629], [3.1629, 3.2302], [3.2302, 3.2975], [3.2975, 3.3648], [3.3648, 3.4321], [3.4321, 3.4994], [3.4994, 3.5667], [3.5667, 3.634], [3.634, 3.7013], [3.7013, 3.7686], [3.7686, 3.8359], [3.8359, 3.9032], [3.9032, 3.9705], [3.9705, 4.0378], [4.0378, 4.1051], [4.1051, 4.1724], [4.1724, 4.2396], [4.2396, 4.3069], [4.3069,